WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Up to 1,200 tons of carbon now leave a single Kahnemara forest each year. This is Unintended

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.140
Consequences, episode 183. In 1948, Sean McBride campaigned for a national target of planting

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.760
10,000 hectares of trees each year to create rural jobs and a domestic timber supply.

00:00:19.716 --> 00:00:26.256
Bulmer Hobson had first outlined the idea in a 1936 article in Ireland today. Hobson argued

00:00:26.256 --> 00:00:31.356
that fast-growing conifers could turn the western gale-tact into a prosperous national forest.

00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:36.516
Sean McBride took up the same goal when he ran in the 1948 general election.

00:00:37.276 --> 00:00:42.116
Later, as Minister for External Affairs, McBride secured cabinet support for that annual

00:00:42.116 --> 00:00:48.456
planting rate. At the time, Ireland planted only about 2,400 hectares a year, and the country

00:00:48.456 --> 00:00:53.756
still imported most of its timber. McBride and his fine-gale colleague, Gerard Sweetman,

00:00:53.756 --> 00:00:58.316
presented the program as a way to bring industry and employment to remote rural districts

00:00:58.316 --> 00:01:03.756
that had few other economic options. The land west of the Shannon was cheap and available.

00:01:04.416 --> 00:01:09.096
Non-native sitka spruce and lodgepole pine were known to grow quickly once established.

00:01:09.656 --> 00:01:14.456
No one at the time measured the carbon stored in the underlying peat, or considered how drainage

00:01:14.456 --> 00:01:19.756
would affect that store. The arithmetic was straightforward. Each additional hectare planted

00:01:19.756 --> 00:01:24.356
had moved Ireland closer to self-sufficiency in wood products while creating measurable

00:01:24.356 --> 00:01:27.436
employment in planting crews, nurseries, and haulage.

00:01:27.876 --> 00:01:33.356
Post-war rural Ireland offered limited alternatives, so directing state funds toward visible

00:01:33.356 --> 00:01:36.656
hectares and jobs made sense on the information than available.

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:43.043
In 1951, the state purchased heavy plows and tractors that allowed foresters to drain deeper

00:01:43.043 --> 00:01:46.123
into blanket bog and higher up the hills than before.

00:01:46.123 --> 00:01:52.163
4. Nearly half the new plantations went on to these western Pete soils. One early site was the

00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:58.203
343 hectare block in the townlands of Dairy Clare and Cluna Carton, in the Inag Valley of

00:01:58.203 --> 00:02:04.383
Canamara. Furls were cut, water was channeled away, and rock phosphate was spread before the spruce

00:02:04.383 --> 00:02:10.623
and pine were planted. The Department of Agriculture later formalized grant schemes that paid up to 100%

00:02:10.623 --> 00:02:16.563
of establishment costs, plus annual premiums for conifer planting. Early reports counted

00:02:16.563 --> 00:02:21.823
the hectares planted and the jobs created in nurseries and fencing crews. Those numbers rose

00:02:21.823 --> 00:02:28.123
steadily through the 1950s and 1960s. The machinery let crews create continuous drainage channels,

00:02:28.703 --> 00:02:33.063
space to keep the root zone aerated long enough for seedlings to survive the first winters.

00:02:33.663 --> 00:02:39.203
Because the grants covered every step from plowing to fencing, landowners faced no direct cost for

00:02:39.203 --> 00:02:44.583
drainage work itself. Success was measured in the annual tally of new hectares and the volume

00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:49.663
of timber projected decades ahead, not in changes to water levels or soil chemistry.

00:02:50.653 --> 00:02:55.953
Drainage lowered the water table across the planted bogs. Oxygen reached the peat, and

00:02:55.953 --> 00:03:01.553
oxidation began. That peat had kept its carbon locked away only while water excluded oxygen.

00:03:02.213 --> 00:03:07.893
The land chosen was the lowest-cost land, and the lowest-cost land was peat. Once drains

00:03:07.893 --> 00:03:13.293
trains lowered the water table by even a few tens of centimeters, microbial activity accelerated,

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:17.533
and the stored carbon began converting to carbon dioxide at a steady rate.

00:03:18.213 --> 00:03:20.713
Tree growth sequesters some carbon above ground.

00:03:21.313 --> 00:03:26.313
The soil emissions continue year after year, whether the timber is harvested or blown down.

00:03:26.893 --> 00:03:30.093
The same drainage networks dried out adjacent unplanted bog.

00:03:30.573 --> 00:03:35.973
The bog lost sponge-like capacity to hold water and to support specialized plants, because

00:03:35.973 --> 00:03:40.733
As the ditches do not stop at plantation boundaries, phosphorus runoff from the fertilizer

00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:46.293
applications acidified streams and harmed Atlantic salmon and freshwater pearl muscle populations.

00:03:46.953 --> 00:03:50.273
The runoff reached water already stressed by that acidification.

00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:56.193
Wind throw increased on the exposed, poorly rooted trees, and the remaining timber often proved

00:03:56.193 --> 00:03:56.853
low value.

00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:03.773
In November 2022, Dr. Kenneth A. Byrne of the University of Limerick told the Joint Committee on Environment

00:04:03.773 --> 00:04:10.673
and climate action that Pete soils make up 38.7% of Irish forests, and that forested blanket

00:04:10.673 --> 00:04:17.033
Pete lands emit 1.68 tons of carbon dioxide per hectare per year. The Irish Times reported

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:24.073
the Kahnemara measurements in 2024. The Irish Independent reported in April 2025 that all Irish

00:04:24.073 --> 00:04:30.213
Pete land habitats already hold unfavorable conservation status, with a forestation identified as

00:04:30.213 --> 00:04:31.393
one of the main drivers.

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:35.893
Enable research noted in March, 2026 that 80% of Irish

00:04:35.893 --> 00:04:38.733
petelands are now degraded, with state-supported

00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:41.193
affora station listed as a continuing pressure.

00:04:42.253 --> 00:04:44.253
Restoration projects now block drains

00:04:44.253 --> 00:04:46.273
and remove trees on selected sites.

00:04:46.873 --> 00:04:50.513
The Irish Times observed in 2024 that felling the conifers

00:04:50.513 --> 00:04:53.233
does not quickly restore the original hydrology

00:04:53.233 --> 00:04:55.013
or the carbon sink function.

00:04:55.573 --> 00:04:58.713
A study of 8,000 hectares of low productivity coil

00:04:58.713 --> 00:05:03.893
to Pete land forests found that re-wedding can temporarily increase emissions before any

00:05:03.893 --> 00:05:05.313
long-term benefit appears.

00:05:05.793 --> 00:05:10.173
Ireland has been referred to the European Union Court of Justice for failing to protect

00:05:10.173 --> 00:05:11.073
bog habitats.

00:05:11.733 --> 00:05:16.413
Current grant schemes still offer payments for some conifer planting on marginal land.

00:05:16.973 --> 00:05:21.673
Researchers continue to weigh whether soil carbon losses on any given site can be offset

00:05:21.673 --> 00:05:22.473
by tree growth.

00:05:23.073 --> 00:05:26.853
The results depend on local drainage patterns and on adjacent land use.

00:05:26.853 --> 00:05:32.173
Blocking drains on one parcel can raise water levels on neighboring farmland, and that

00:05:32.173 --> 00:05:35.753
rise creates new conflicts over whether the land can still be used.

00:05:36.213 --> 00:05:40.673
The same grant structure that once rewarded drainage now funds some restoration work.

00:05:41.093 --> 00:05:46.153
The rule of paying per hectare planted remains in place for other forest types, a subsidy

00:05:46.153 --> 00:05:51.633
that counts hectares and projected volume and does not count soil carbon leaves the decisive

00:05:51.633 --> 00:05:52.913
cost off the ledger.

00:05:52.913 --> 00:05:58.493
The test has to use the soil that will actually be paid for, not a national average that hides

00:05:58.493 --> 00:05:59.333
the wet ground.

00:05:59.773 --> 00:06:04.193
Whoever approves the payment has to see the carbon and water result for that exact soil

00:06:04.193 --> 00:06:06.233
type before the first grant is approved.

00:06:06.893 --> 00:06:11.873
Environmental intelligence are sibling briefing on regulation and the living world, follows

00:06:11.873 --> 00:06:14.993
how measurements like these enter a rule or stay outside it.

00:06:15.453 --> 00:06:20.553
Before the next payment for trees, restoration, or a carbon claim is built only around area,

00:06:20.553 --> 00:06:25.233
What published change in water and soil carbon would you require on that ground first?

00:06:25.713 --> 00:06:26.913
That's the case for today.

